# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_pager_create(vx_handle resource, vx_handle port, uint64_t key,
                          vx_duration deadline, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_vmo_create_pager(vx_handle pager, uint32_t key, uint64_t size,
                              vx_handle *out);

vx_status vx_pager_supply(vx_handle pager, vx_handle vmo, uint64_t offset,
                          uint64_t size, vx_handle source,
                          uint64_t source_offset);

int64_t vx_pager_op(vx_handle pager, vx_handle vmo, uint32_t op,
                    uint64_t offset, uint64_t size, vx_pager_range *ranges);
vx_status vx_pager_resize(vx_handle pager, vx_handle vmo, uint64_t size);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A pager is a trusted task's supply of pages for VMOs (see vmo(2)), how a file server backs the mapping of files: `fsd` is the system's pager, and answers a `Tmap` with a pager-backed VMO holding the file, one for all who map it (docs/11 §8).
Pagers are trusted or absent: only a task that `svcd` has marked a pager holds the authority to make one.

`vx_pager_create` makes a Pager and puts a handle to it in <out>.
<resource> must be a Resource handle with `VX_RIGHT_PAGER`, which `svcd` gives, narrowed to that right alone, to a service whose manifest says `pager`; one with `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`, the root Resource, serves as well.
Page requests go to <port>, which needs `VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL`, as packets whose key is <key>.
<deadline> is how long, in nanoseconds, a thread that touches a missing page waits for it; it may not be 0.
The handle has `VX_RIGHT_READ`, `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, `VX_RIGHT_DUPLICATE`, `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` and `VX_RIGHT_INSPECT`; every call on the Pager needs `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.
The Pager holds its port, and each of its VMOs holds the Pager.

`vx_vmo_create_pager`, which vmo(2) describes, makes a VMO whose pages the Pager supplies, none yet, named in requests by <key>.
The pager keeps a handle to it, and hands others out as it chooses.

## Faults

A mapping of a pager-backed VMO maps what has been supplied when it is made, and the rest as it is touched.
A thread that touches a page not supplied faults, and the kernel asks the pager for it with a packet on its port: trigger `VX_TRIGGER_PAGER`, key the Pager's key, source the VMO's key, and value the page's range, read with `vx_pager_offset` and `vx_pager_pages`.
It asks once however many threads fault on the page, and each waits.
When the page comes, the waiters map it and go on; when the pager's deadline passes first, counted from a thread's first wait, the thread takes a `VX_EXCEPTION_PAGER_TIMEOUT` exception instead, naming the page and whether it read, wrote or executed (POSIX's `SIGBUS`), and the next fault on the page asks again.
A request the port has no room for is not lost: the faulting thread asks again every millisecond until its deadline.

A page is mapped read-only, even in a writable mapping, until it is written: that write faults, marks the page dirty and maps it writable, which is how the pager learns what to write back.
A forked task shares its parent's mappings of a pager-backed VMO rather than copying them.
The kernel's own copies to and from user memory take no page that is not there yet; they fail as on an unmapped page, so a program touches a mapped file's pages itself before handing them to a system call.

## Supplying

`vx_pager_supply` copies pages <source_offset> to <source_offset> + <size> of <source> into pages <offset> to <offset> + <size> of <vmo>, and wakes every thread waiting on <vmo>'s pages.
<vmo> must be one of <pager>'s, and needs no rights of its own; <source> must be an anonymous VMO, with `VX_RIGHT_READ`.
The offsets and <size> must be page-aligned, <size> not zero, and both ranges within their VMOs.
A page already supplied stays as it is, and the copy of it is dropped.
A page need not have been asked for: a pager may supply pages ahead of the faults that would ask.

## Operations

`vx_pager_op` acts on pages <offset> to <offset> + <size> of <vmo>, which must be one of <pager>'s.
<offset> and <size> must be page-aligned; pages of the range past the VMO's end are ignored.
<op> is one of:

: `VX_PAGER_DIRTY`
  The range's dirty pages, as runs of adjacent pages, into <ranges>: at most `VX_PAGER_RANGES` (64) `vx_pager_range`, each an offset and a size in bytes.
  It returns how many it wrote, so 64 may mean more are left: the pager cleans those it has, and asks again.
  A page is dirty from its first write after it was supplied or cleaned.
: `VX_PAGER_CLEAN`
  The range's pages clean, and taken out of every mapping, so the next write to one marks it dirty again.
  To write back, a pager cleans a range, then reads it and writes it out: a write before the clean is in what it reads, one after it is dirty for next time.
  <ranges> is not used.
: `VX_PAGER_EVICT`
  The range's clean pages taken out of every mapping and freed, absent again: a touch asks the pager for them as before.
  Dirty pages stay.
: `VX_PAGER_IDLE`
  Returns 1 if the caller's handle to <vmo> is its only reference, with no other handle and no mapping, and 0 otherwise.
  The pager may then let the VMO go, as `fsd` does with a file no one maps.
  <offset> and <size> are 0.
: `VX_PAGER_RESIZE`
  The VMO's new size is <size>, rounded up to a page, with <offset> 0; `vx_pager_resize` makes this call.
  Pages added are absent.
  Pages past a smaller size are made absent, taken out of every mapping and freed, and a touch there, or a mapping made there from then on, is refused as past the VMO's end.
  Resizing is the pager's alone, since everyone who maps the VMO shares its size; `vmo_op` on a pager-backed VMO is refused.

# EXAMPLES

`fsd` answers a page request: each page read from the file into an anonymous scratch page, zeros past the file's end, and supplied from it (`servers/fsd/fsd.c`, lines rewrapped):

```c
static void supply(const vx_packet *pk) {
  uint32_t i = (uint32_t)pk->source;
  if (i >= MAX_CACHED || !pcache[i].used) return;
  cached *c = &pcache[i];
  vxfs_file f;
  bool there = !c->dead && file_of(c->node, &f) == VX_OK;
  uint64_t first = vx_pager_offset(pk->value);
  for (uint64_t p = 0; p < vx_pager_pages(pk->value); p++) {
    uint64_t at = first + p * 4096, got = 0;
    if (there && at < f.d.length)
      vxfs_read(&vol, tree_of(c->node), &f, at, page_buf, 4096, &got);
    memset(page_buf + got, 0, 4096 - got);
    if (vx_vmo_rw(scratch, VX_VMO_WRITE, 0, page_buf, 4096) == VX_OK)
      vx_pager_supply(pager, c->vmo, at, 4096, scratch, 0);
  }
}
```

And writes back what the mappings wrote, asking for the dirty ranges until there are none, each set cleaned before it is read:

```c
for (uint32_t round = 0; round < 4096; round++) {
  vx_pager_range ranges[VX_PAGER_RANGES];
  int64_t n = vx_pager_op(pager, c->vmo, VX_PAGER_DIRTY, 0, c->size,
                          ranges);
  if (n <= 0) return;
  writeback_ranges(c, &f, ranges, n);
}
```

