# DIAGNOSTICS

`vx_pager_create` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  The Pager is made, and its handle is in <out>.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <resource> is not a handle to a Resource, or <port> not one to a Port.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <resource> has neither `VX_RIGHT_PAGER` nor `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE`, or <port> lacks `VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL`.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <deadline> is 0, or <out> is not writable memory of the caller's.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  There is no memory for the Pager or its handle.

`vx_pager_supply` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  The pages are supplied.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <pager>, <vmo> or <source> is not a handle to an object of its kind.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <pager> lacks `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`, or <source> lacks `VX_RIGHT_READ`.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <vmo> is not one of <pager>'s VMOs.
: `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`
  <source> is physical or pager-backed.
: `VX_ERR_RANGE`
  <size> is 0, an offset or <size> is not page-aligned, or a range does not lie within its VMO.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  There was no memory for a page; the pages before it are supplied, and the waiters woken.

`vx_pager_op` returns a count for `VX_PAGER_DIRTY`, 1 or 0 for `VX_PAGER_IDLE`, `VX_OK` for the others, or:

: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <op> is not one of the five, <offset> or <size> is not page-aligned, <ranges> is not writable memory for 64 ranges when <op> is `VX_PAGER_DIRTY`, <vmo> is not one of <pager>'s VMOs, or <offset> is not 0 for `VX_PAGER_RESIZE`.
: `VX_ERR_RANGE`
  <offset> + <size> wraps, or for `VX_PAGER_RESIZE` <size> is 0 or more than 256 MiB.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`
  <pager> or <vmo> is not a handle to an object of its kind.
: `VX_ERR_ACCESS`
  <pager> lacks `VX_RIGHT_WRITE`.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE`
  Another resize of the VMO is under way.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  There is no memory for a larger page list.

# BUGS

The design's access hints (`willneed`, `sequential`, `random`, `dontneed`; 01 §5, docs/11 §8) are not there, and the kernel asks for one page at a time: a request's range is always a single page, though its value can say more.

The kernel does not yet take clean pages of pager-backed VMOs back under memory pressure on its own; only `VX_PAGER_EVICT` frees them.

`VX_PAGER_IDLE` counts references, so a reference the kernel holds for a moment, such as one a call in progress on the VMO holds, reads as not idle.

A pager that dies leaves its VMOs: their supplied pages stay, and a fault on an absent page waits out the deadline and takes `VX_EXCEPTION_PAGER_TIMEOUT`.
